  "summary": "The dramatic performance technique where performers change masks in quick succession without apparent movement.",
  "description": "Bianlian involves performers rapidly changing colorful painted masks or faces through sleight of hand, hidden mechanisms, or costume manipulation, creating moments of surprise and transformation. This spectacular technique developed in Sichuan opera during the late Qing Dynasty as a way to express character transformations and supernatural abilities, becoming the signature element of Sichuan opera performances.",
  "culturalContext": "Bianlian — Sichuan opera's \"face-changing\" stunt — swaps painted silk masks in fractions of second: the performer's brush-flick, fan-pass or cloak-toss transforms character before the eye, with up to 24 faces in legend masters (Peng Denghuai). A guarded trade secret (state-protected \"second-class secret\"), bianlian tours globally with Faye Wong's film cameos and Spring Festival galas; its sudden-color logic has inspired designers, magicians and cartoon transitions alike.",
